    <h2 id="s1">The short answer</h2>
    <p>An Iceland heli-ski holiday is a multi-day trip, usually three to five days, built around helicopter access to untracked mountains on the <strong>Troll Peninsula in North Iceland</strong>, with nights spent at a four-star lodge on the fjord. You fly out to reach terrain no lift can touch, ski long descents that finish near the Arctic Ocean, and return each evening to proper comfort in the town of Siglufjörður.</p>
    <p>The planning comes down to five decisions: when to go, how long to stay, which package format suits your group, who is coming and what you need to bring. Get those right and the rest is handled for you. Heliski Travel arranges the whole trip end to end, and as the authorised agent, booking through us costs exactly the same as going direct.</p>

    <h2 id="s2">What a holiday includes</h2>
    <p>A heli-ski holiday is close to all-inclusive on the mountain side, which is part of what makes it feel effortless once you arrive. A typical package covers:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Helicopter flying and guaranteed vertical feet</strong>, the core of the trip, priced by descent rather than by the day.</li>
      <li><strong>IFMGA/UIAGM-certified mountain guiding</strong> in small groups, typically four guests to a guide.</li>
      <li>A full <strong>BCA avalanche safety kit</strong> per guest, plus <strong>K2 skis or boards</strong> if you would rather travel light.</li>
      <li><strong>Accommodation and meals at the four-star Sigló Hótel</strong> in Siglufjörður.</li>
      <li>Transfers and the daily logistics of getting you from lodge to mountain and back.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>What you bring is short: your own boots and helmet, ski clothing, and travel insurance that explicitly covers off-piste and helicopter activity. Everything else is arranged. For the fine print on what drives the figure, see our guide to <a href="/heliskiing-guide/heliskiing-cost-iceland">what heli-skiing costs in Iceland</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s3">When to go</h2>
    <p>The Iceland heli-ski season runs <strong>March to mid-June</strong>, and the character of the holiday shifts across it. Early season, in <strong>March and April</strong>, brings deep, cold, wintry snow and a proper powder feel. Late season, in <strong>May and early June</strong>, trades some of that depth for two things Iceland does like almost nowhere else: extraordinarily long daylight and, at the very end, midnight-sun skiing.</p>
    <p>For most holidaymakers the sweet spot is April to mid-May, where you get a strong chance of good snow alongside long, generous ski days. If the idea of skiing under a sun that barely sets appeals, aim for late May or June. Either way, the long Arctic daylight means more skiable hours per day than a mid-winter destination can offer. Read more on <a href="/heliskiing-guide/best-time-to-heliski-iceland">the best time to heli-ski in Iceland</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s4">How long to stay</h2>
    <p>Holidays here are built as <strong>three, four and five-day weeks</strong>, and longer is generally better for one simple reason: weather. Heli-skiing depends on flyable conditions, and a longer stay gives the sky more chances to cooperate. A single day is a gamble; a proper week almost always delivers.</p>
    <p>The pricing model protects you here too. Because trips are sold by <strong>guaranteed vertical feet</strong> rather than by the day, a storm that grounds the fleet carries your vertical over rather than wasting it. That turns a longer holiday from a risk into an insurance policy: more days, more buffer, more skiing. Factor in the travel to North Iceland and a four or five-day trip makes far more sense than a flying visit.</p>

    <h2 id="s5">Where you are based</h2>
    <p>Your base for the holiday is the <strong>four-star Sigló Hótel</strong>, built onto the marina in <strong>Siglufjörður</strong>, once the herring capital of the North Atlantic and now one of Iceland's most characterful small towns. It is a short helicopter hop from the terrain, which means more of your day is spent skiing and less of it in transit.</p>
    <p>The evenings are part of the holiday, not an afterthought. You finish a run near the ocean, fly back over the fjord and step into proper rooms, a restaurant and a harbour-side hot tub with the water and peaks in front of you. It is the contrast, serious mountains by day and genuine comfort by night, that people remember. See more on <a href="/siglo-hotel">the Sigló Hótel</a> and <a href="/field-notes/the-heliski-lodge-experience">the lodge experience</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s6">Who it suits</h2>
    <p>An Iceland heli-ski holiday suits confident off-piste skiers and riders who want an extraordinary trip without organising the logistics themselves. You do not need to be an expert, but you should be a <strong>competent, confident intermediate</strong> who can ski most red terrain comfortably and link turns in variable snow.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Couples and small groups of friends marking a milestone or a long-planned adventure.</li>
      <li>Mixed-ability groups, since guides can shape terrain from mellow to steep across the day.</li>
      <li>Skiers who have done the marquee resorts and want something no lift can offer.</li>
      <li>Anyone who values the experience over the price tag and would ski it hard.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you are newer to off-piste, it is worth reading our note on <a href="/field-notes/heliskiing-for-beginners">heli-skiing for beginners</a> before you commit, so the holiday is a joy rather than a stretch.</p>

    <h2 id="s7">What it costs</h2>
    <p>Let us be direct about the number. Our Iceland heli-ski packages run <strong>roughly EUR 3,490 to EUR 82,990</strong>, covering three, four and five-day weeks in shared, semi-private and private formats. The <strong>format you choose moves the price far more than the dates do</strong>. A shared group on a shorter week sits at the lower end; a private helicopter for a full week sits at the top.</p>
    <p>Crucially, the pricing is honest about weather. Trips are sold by guaranteed vertical feet, so delays and repositioning are the operator's cost, not yours. That removes the single worst way a premium holiday can go wrong. For a full breakdown and a look at the vertical-feet model, see <a href="/field-notes/iceland-heliskiing-cost-breakdown">the Iceland heli-ski cost breakdown</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s8">Shared, semi-private or private</h2>
    <p>The format is the biggest single decision, and each level is a genuinely good holiday in its own right.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Shared group.</strong> You fly with other guests, spreading the helicopter cost. The most value-efficient way to ski untracked terrain.</li>
      <li><strong>Semi-private.</strong> A smaller, more controlled group with more flexibility, while still sharing some cost. A sensible middle for couples and small parties.</li>
      <li><strong>Private.</strong> The helicopter is reserved for your group alone, with maximum flexibility on pace and terrain. The fullest expression of the holiday.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Our comparison of <a href="/field-notes/private-vs-shared-helicopter-heliskiing">private versus shared helicopters</a> weighs the trade-offs so you can spend where it counts for your group.</p>

    <h2 id="s9">What to bring</h2>
    <p>The packing list is refreshingly short because so much is provided. Bring your own <strong>ski or snowboard boots and helmet</strong>, warm and waterproof ski clothing in layers, gloves, goggles and sunglasses, and a small daypack. Skis and boards can be provided if you prefer to travel light.</p>
    <p>The one non-negotiable is <strong>travel insurance that explicitly covers off-piste and helicopter activity</strong>, as standard ski policies often exclude both. Come fit, too: the fitter you are, the more of your guaranteed vertical you will actually ski. Our full <a href="/field-notes/what-to-pack-for-heliskiing">heli-skiing packing guide</a> covers everything in detail.</p>

    <h2 id="s10">How to book</h2>
    <p>Booking a heli-ski holiday should be simple, and with Heliski Travel it is. Tell us your group size, your skiing level, your preferred dates and the format you have in mind, and we will lay out the honest options and handle the arrangements end to end. As the <strong>authorised booking agent for Viking Heliskiing</strong>, booking through us costs exactly the same as going direct, and we reply within 12 hours.</p>
    <p>When you are ready, <a href="/packages">browse the packages</a>, see what a week looks like on the <a href="/iceland">Iceland page</a>, or <a href="/contact">request a tailored quote</a>. Tell us what you are picturing and we will build the holiday around it.</p>

    <h2 id="s11">A typical day on your holiday</h2>
    <p>It helps to picture the rhythm of a day, because it is more relaxed than the adrenaline suggests. Mornings begin with breakfast at the lodge and a <strong>weather and avalanche briefing</strong>, followed by a check of your safety kit. When the guide gives the word, the helicopter lifts your small group to the first drop, a ridgeline or summit chosen for the day's conditions and light.</p>
    <p>From there the day settles into a satisfying loop: the guide leads a long, untracked descent, the helicopter meets you at the bottom, and you fly back up for the next run. There is a pause somewhere spectacular for a <strong>mountain lunch</strong>, then more skiing through the afternoon. A typical day delivers <strong>15,000 to 25,000 vertical feet across 7 to 14 runs</strong>. By evening you are back at the Sigló Hótel, boots off, watching the light move across the fjord. It is a full day of skiing wrapped in comfort at both ends.</p>

    <h2 id="s12">Combining it with the rest of Iceland</h2>
    <p>Many guests turn the trip into a broader Icelandic holiday, and it works beautifully. Because you fly into Keflavík near Reykjavík before travelling north, it is easy to add a day or two in the capital or on the Golden Circle at the start or end of your week, then head up to Siglufjörður for the skiing itself.</p>
    <p>The journey north is part of the experience: a domestic flight or scenic drive to Akureyri, then on to Siglufjörður through some of Iceland's most dramatic coastal scenery. If you are travelling with a non-skiing partner, the town and region offer plenty, from the herring-era heritage to hot pools and fjord walks. Our guide to <a href="/field-notes/getting-to-north-iceland-heliskiing">getting to North Iceland</a> covers the logistics, and the <a href="/travel-here">travel page</a> has the practical detail.</p>

